      "scale": [
        1,
        1
      ],
      "crop": null
    },
    {
      "id": "pJxyG8qtqcxx5uywL2M-n",
      "type": "text",
      "x": 264.265625,
      "y": 2749.28125,
      "width": 800,
      "height": 175,
      "angle": 0,
      "strokeColor": "#1e1e1e",
      "backgroundColor": "transparent",
      "fillStyle": "solid",
      "strokeWidth": 2,
      "strokeStyle": "solid",
      "roughness": 1,
      "opacity": 100,
      "groupIds": [],
      "frameId": null,
      "index": "ah",
      "roundness": null,
      "seed": 959228926,
      "version": 4,
      "versionNonce": 1616053438,
      "isDeleted": false,
      "boundElements": [],
      "updated": 1793270932624,
      "created": 1793270924226,
      "link": null,
      "locked": false,
      "text": "An interesting take on RAG for chatbots is MemoryBank, a mechanism that\nallows LLMs to recall relevant memories as a long-term mechanism (external\ndatabase) rather than a short-term mechanism (conversation history). Its\nexperiences through conversations are stored in a separate database that allows\nthe LLM to retrieve relevant memories. What sets it apart from regular RAG is\nthat this memory is continuously updated to selectively preserve memory through\nan updating mechanism.",
      "fontSize": 20,
      "baseFontSize": null,
      "fontFamily": 5,
      "textAlign": "left",
      "verticalAlign": "top",
      "containerId": null,
      "originalText": "An interesting take on RAG for chatbots is MemoryBank, a mechanism that allows LLMs to recall relevant memories as a long-term mechanism (external database) rather than a short-term mechanism (conversation history). Its experiences through conversations are stored in a separate database that allows the LLM to retrieve relevant memories. What sets it apart from regular RAG is that this memory is continuously updated to selectively preserve memory through an updating mechanism.",
      "autoResize": false,
      "lineHeight": 1.25,
      "labelPosition": null
    },
    {
      "id": "hyBeBg5EcE2aHQ0qDYGbo",
      "type": "text",
      "x": 276.265625,
      "y": 2929.9375,
      "width": 800,
      "height": 350,
      "angle": 0,
      "strokeColor": "#1e1e1e",
      "backgroundColor": "transparent",
      "fillStyle": "solid",
      "strokeWidth": 2,
      "strokeStyle": "solid",
      "roughness": 1,
      "opacity": 100,
      "groupIds": [],
      "frameId": null,
      "index": "ai",
      "roundness": null,
      "seed": 643664226,
      "version": 91,
      "versionNonce": 1881703138,
      "isDeleted": false,
      "boundElements": [],
      "updated": 1793271815388,
      "created": 1793271799840,
      "link": null,
      "locked": false,
      "text": "This mechanism allows the MemoryBank to forget and reinforce memory inspired\nby the Ebbinghaus Forgetting Curve theory, which is a curve demonstrating the\npace at which we tend to forget. The curve is often shown as being exponential,\nresulting in a loss of half of what we learn each day. A common way to prevent\nforgetting what you learned, for instance when preparing for exams, is to actively\nrecall the learned information frequently. This is referred to as spaced repetition,\nwhich tends to decrease the pace at which knowledge is forgotten.\n\nMemoryBank borrows from this theory and frequently updates the long-term\nmemory of an LLM based on which pieces of knowledge are (not) accessed.\nSpecifically, this means that when a memory item is retrieved and used during\nconversations, it will persist longer in the MemoryBank. However, if the memory\nitem hasn’t been retrieved for a while, then there is a chance the memory will be\nremoved entirely.",
      "fontSize": 20,
      "baseFontSize": null,
      "fontFamily": 5,
      "textAlign": "left",
      "verticalAlign": "top",
      "containerId": null,
      "originalText": "This mechanism allows the MemoryBank to forget and reinforce memory inspired by the Ebbinghaus Forgetting Curve theory, which is a curve demonstrating the pace at which we tend to forget. The curve is often shown as being exponential, resulting in a loss of half of what we learn each day. A common way to prevent forgetting what you learned, for instance when preparing for exams, is to actively recall the learned information frequently. This is referred to as spaced repetition, which tends to decrease the pace at which knowledge is forgotten.\n\nMemoryBank borrows from this theory and frequently updates the long-term memory of an LLM based on which pieces of knowledge are (not) accessed. Specifically, this means that when a memory item is retrieved and used during conversations, it will persist longer in the MemoryBank. However, if the memory item hasn’t been retrieved for a while, then there is a chance the memory will be removed entirely.",
      "autoResize": false,
      "lineHeight": 1.25,
      "labelPosition": null
    },
    {
      "id": "j6Ef_BHA29OlDS81Fmnu1",
      "type": "text",
      "x": 275.265625,
      "y": 3278.484375,
      "width": 800,
      "height": 800,
      "angle": 0,
      "strokeColor": "#1e1e1e",
      "backgroundColor": "transparent",
      "fillStyle": "solid",
      "strokeWidth": 2,
      "strokeStyle": "solid",
      "roughness": 1,
      "opacity": 100,
      "groupIds": [],
      "frameId": null,
      "index": "aj",
      "roundness": null,
      "seed": 1018524002,
      "version": 3,
      "versionNonce": 825098466,
      "isDeleted": false,
      "boundElements": [],
      "updated": 1793271844902,
      "created": 1793271843584,
      "link": null,
      "locked": false,
      "text": "he authors use a few variants of memory:\n\nConversation history\n\n    Raw multi-turn conversations.\nSummaries of past events\n\n    These are generated by an LLM based on the conversation history.\nUser’s portrait\n\n    The personality traits and emotions of the user as summarized by the LLM\nbased on the conversation history.\n\nThese forms of memory are used to create the MemoryBank, as shown in Figure\n4-17, and show how an LLM may create the summaries of past events as well as\nthe user’s portrait.\n\nThe summaries and conversation turns are embedded so that they can easily be\nretrieved. The user portrait is dynamically updated and always passed as\nadditional context. Figure 4-18 shows a full overview of this pipeline. When a\nquery is created, it is embedded, and related conversation turns and summaries\nare retrieved, together with the user portrait. When conversation turns are\nretrieved, their strength is updated, making them less likely to be removed from\nthe MemoryBank. The retrieved context, together with the query, is used as\ninput for the LLM to generate an answer.\n\nThis form of memory demonstrates the potential complexity of RAG-like\napplications, where each use case necessitates different types of memories,\nsummarizations, etc. As such, there are many forms of RAG, such as GraphRAG\nand Multi-modal RAG, that each require its own set of considerations. The RAG\nexamples shown previously are often referred to as vanilla RAG or naive RAG for\ntheir straightforward implementation.",
      "fontSize": 20,
      "baseFontSize": null,
      "fontFamily": 5,
      "textAlign": "left",
      "verticalAlign": "top",
      "containerId": null,
      "originalText": "he authors use a few variants of memory:\n\nConversation history\n\n    Raw multi-turn conversations.\nSummaries of past events\n\n    These are generated by an LLM based on the conversation history.\nUser’s portrait\n\n    The personality traits and emotions of the user as summarized by the LLM based on the conversation history.\n\nThese forms of memory are used to create the MemoryBank, as shown in Figure 4-17, and show how an LLM may create the summaries of past events as well as the user’s portrait.\n\nThe summaries and conversation turns are embedded so that they can easily be retrieved. The user portrait is dynamically updated and always passed as additional context. Figure 4-18 shows a full overview of this pipeline. When a query is created, it is embedded, and related conversation turns and summaries are retrieved, together with the user portrait. When conversation turns are retrieved, their strength is updated, making them less likely to be removed from the MemoryBank. The retrieved context, together with the query, is used as input for the LLM to generate an answer.\n\nThis form of memory demonstrates the potential complexity of RAG-like applications, where each use case necessitates different types of memories, summarizations, etc. As such, there are many forms of RAG, such as GraphRAG and Multi-modal RAG, that each require its own set of considerations. The RAG examples shown previously are often referred to as vanilla RAG or naive RAG for their straightforward implementation.",
      "autoResize": false,
      "lineHeight": 1.25,
      "labelPosition": null
    },
